{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "State Newton's Second Law of Motion in words."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to recall the relationship between net force, mass, and acceleration, and the direction of the resulting motion. A single focused diagnostic question will reveal whether they understand the directionality and proportionality before they attempt a full statement.",
      "content": "If a net force pushes an object to the right, which way does the object accelerate? Right, left, or somewhere else?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "When a net force acts on an object, the object will accelerate opposite to the direction of the force, and the acceleration is directly proportional to the mass and inversely proportional to the force."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has swapped the direction (acceleration opposite the force) and inverted the proportionality (mass ↔ force). This is a classic sign‑direction error and a reversal of the proportionality relationship. I will point out each mistake, ask a confirming question, and then guide them to the correct wording. Finally I will give the full answer with a formula breakdown and exam tip.",
      "content": "I see two points where the statement got flipped:\n\n1. **Direction** – Newton's Second Law says the acceleration is *in the same direction* as the net force, not opposite. Think of pushing a sled forward; it moves forward, not backward.\n2. **Proportionality** – The acceleration grows when the force gets larger (directly proportional to **force**) and gets smaller when the mass gets larger (inversely proportional to **mass**). It’s the opposite of what you wrote.\n\nLet’s correct each part step‑by‑step. First, can you restate the direction relationship in your own words?\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}} = m\\mathbf{a}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}}$: the total (resultant) force acting on the object, pointing in the direction the force is applied.\n  * $m$: the mass of the object – a measure of how much matter it contains.\n  * $\\mathbf{a}$: the acceleration of the object, which points **the same way** as $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}}$.\n* **Why we use it:** It tells us that a bigger push (larger force) makes the object speed up more, while a heavier object (larger mass) resists that change. The law links cause (force) to effect (acceleration) in a clear, quantitative way.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In DBE exams, always include the direction (\"in the direction of the net force\") and the correct proportionality wording (\"directly proportional to the force and inversely proportional to the mass\"). Missing either part can cost you a mark for the definition.\n\nWhen you’re ready, write the full statement again using the corrected ideas."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "error_analysis",
    "final_answer": "When a (non-zero) resultant/net force acts on an object, the object will accelerate in the direction of the force with an acceleration that is directly proportional to the force and inversely proportional to the mass of the object.",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nanswer = \"When a (non-zero) resultant/net force acts on an object, the object will accelerate in the direction of the force with an acceleration that is directly proportional to the force and inversely proportional to the mass of the object.\"\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": ["error_analysis", "conceptual_check"]
  }
}

{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Rocket A (mass 3m) and Rocket B (mass 2m) are moving upwards together at velocity v. After an internal explosion, Rocket A moves downwards at 1/3 v. State the principle of conservation of momentum in words. (2 marks)"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "This is a conceptual definition question. The most common error in this question is that learners write only 'momentum before equals momentum after' and forget the essential condition 'in an isolated/closed system'. I need to diagnose whether the learner knows that condition without giving the full answer away. So I ask one small guiding question: what must be true about the system for total momentum to be conserved?",
      "content": "Let's focus on the wording of the principle. The formula `p_before = p_after` is often how we use the principle, but when we *state the principle in words*, there is a special condition about the system that must be included.\n\nCan you tell me: what must be true about the system for its total momentum to remain constant?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The momentum before the explosion is equal to the momentum after the explosion."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has the 'before = after' idea, which is correct as the main consequence, but they have omitted the crucial condition 'isolated/closed system'. In the CAPS memo, this condition carries the second mark. I need to affirm the correct part and then give the full, memo-accurate statement, followed by a simple conceptual breakdown and exam tip.",
      "content": "The before/after equality is one part of the idea, but the full principle needs a condition: the system must be **isolated**.\n\nThe correct statement from the memo is:\n\n> **The total linear momentum of an isolated system remains constant.**\n\nWithout the word *isolated*, the statement is incomplete, because external forces (like friction or an outside thrust) can change the total momentum of a system.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(\\sum \\vec{p}_{\\text{before}} = \\sum \\vec{p}_{\\text{after}}\\), where \\(\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}\\).\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(\\vec{p}\\): momentum of a single object — its \"quantity of motion\".\n  * \\(m\\): the mass of the object, in kilograms (kg).\n  * \\(\\vec{v}\\): the velocity of the object, in metres per second (m·s⁻¹).\n  * \\(\\sum\\): \"sum of\" — add up the momentum of every object in the system.\n* **Why we use it:** In an isolated system, no external force acts on the system. The explosion is an *internal* interaction, so Rocket A and Rocket B push on each other with equal and opposite forces. Momentum is transferred between them, but the total momentum of the system stays the same. That is why conservation of momentum works here.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** For a 2-mark \"state the principle\" question, you need **both** parts: (1) total momentum remains constant, and (2) the system is isolated/closed. Many learners write only \"momentum before equals momentum after\" and lose the condition mark. So always include \"in an isolated system\"."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "error_analysis",
    "final_answer": "The total linear momentum of an isolated system remains constant.",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\np_before, p_after = sp.symbols('p_before p_after')\neq = sp.Eq(p_before, p_after)\nprint('The total linear momentum of an isolated system remains constant.')",
    "techniques": ["predict_observe_explain", "identify_missing_condition"]
  }
}

