  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Define the term power in words. (2 marks)"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student needs the word-definition of power. They likely remember the word 'work' but may be unsure how time fits in. Fast-track approach: ask one diagnostic fill-in-the-blank question to guide them to the key word 'rate'. No calculation is needed here, so no numeric tool call is required. [TOOL CHECK: board(text=\"Power is the ____ at which work is done.\") -> shows blank]",
      "content": "Power is about how quickly work is done. Try completing this sentence:\n\n\"Power is the ____ at which work is done.\"\n\nWhat word goes in the blank?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It is the rate at which work is done."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student gave the correct key word 'rate' and correctly linked it to work done. Now I can confirm the full definition, add the energy-expended alternative, give the formula breakdown, and point out the 2-mark exam expectation. No calculation needed. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"P = W / delta_t\") -> symbolic relationship]",
      "content": "Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is expended. ✅\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(P = \\frac{W}{\\Delta t}\\)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(P\\): power, measured in watts (W) — how fast work is done or energy is transferred.\n  * \\(W\\): work done or energy transferred, measured in joules (J).\n  * \\(\\Delta t\\): the time taken, measured in seconds (s).\n* **Why we use it:** Power is not the total amount of work; it is the work divided by the time taken. For example, running up stairs uses more power than walking up the same stairs, even though the total work done is the same.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** This is a 2-mark definition. You need **both** key ideas: the word **rate** and the idea of **work done / energy expended**. Saying only \"work done\" loses one mark, so always use the phrase \"the rate at which work is done or energy is expended.\""
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "The rate at which work is done or energy is expended.",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nanswer = \"The rate at which work is done or energy is expended.\"\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": ["concept_map", "definition_breakdown"]
  }
}

